Overlapping matches are being dropped by this string searcher and nobody noticed until the bioinformatics team complained. searcher.py, verbatim:

def prefix_function(p):
    f = [0] * len(p)
    k = 0
    for i in range(1, len(p)):
        while k > 0 and p[i] != p[k]:
            k = f[k - 1]
        if p[i] == p[k]:
            k += 1
        f[i] = k
    return f

def find_all(text, pat):
    if not pat:
        return []
    f = prefix_function(pat)
    res = []
    j = 0
    for i, c in enumerate(text):
        while j > 0 and c != pat[j]:
            j = f[j - 1]
        if c == pat[j]:
            j += 1
        if j == len(pat):
            res.append(i - j + 1)
            j = 0
    return res

The prefix function is fine; the search loop loses overlapping occurrences after each hit. First: a failing pytest test — find_all("aaaa", "aa") should be [0, 1, 2]; show the red run. Then fix the post-match state (what should j become after a match?), and harden the suite: fuzz vs a str.find-loop oracle on 800 seeded random "ab"-alphabet cases, pattern == text, pattern of length 1, and match at the very end of text. Finish with pytest fully green.

